---
import FoundationPage from "../components/FoundationPage.astro";
import "@skryensya/core/components/table.css";
import CodeBlock from "../components/CodeBlock.astro";

const example = `import { useForm } from "react-hook-form";
import { zodResolver } from "@hookform/resolvers/zod";
import { z } from "zod";
import { ControlledField, Form } from "@skryensya/react/hook-form";
import { Input } from "@skryensya/react/input";
import { Button } from "@skryensya/react/button";

const schema = z.object({ email: z.string().email("Enter a valid email.") });

export function Signup() {
  const form = useForm({ resolver: zodResolver(schema), defaultValues: { email: "" } });
  return (
    <Form form={form} onSubmit={(values) => save(values)}>
      <ControlledField name="email" label="Email" required>
        {({ bind }) => <Input type="email" {...bind.input()} />}
      </ControlledField>
      <Button type="submit">Create account</Button>
    </Form>
  );
}`;

const bindings = [
  ["Input, Textarea, SelectNative", "bind.input()", "A string"],
  ["Checkbox, Switch", "bind.checked()", "A boolean"],
  ["Select, Combobox, TagsInput, DatePicker", "bind.value()", "A list of strings"],
  ["SegmentedControl, RadioGroup, OtpInput, ColorPicker", "bind.value()", "A string"],
  ["Slider, Rating", "bind.value()", "A number"],
];
---

<FoundationPage
  title="React Hook Form"
  description="Use React Hook Form for the value and validation of a form, and FormField for how each field looks and reads."
>
  <p class="lede">
    The library decides what a field holds and whether it is valid. This system decides how the field
    looks and what a screen reader hears. The integration is the seam between the two: it hands the
    error to the field and the value to the control, and decides nothing about either.
  </p>

  <h2>Who owns what</h2>
  <div class="sk-table-scroll">
    <table class="sk-table">
      <thead class="sk-table__head">
        <tr class="sk-table__row">
          <th class="sk-table__header">React Hook Form</th>
          <th class="sk-table__header">This system</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody class="sk-table__body">
        <tr class="sk-table__row">
          <td class="sk-table__cell">The value of each field and the default values</td>
          <td class="sk-table__cell">The label, the hint and the error, and the ids that tie them to the control</td>
        </tr>
        <tr class="sk-table__row">
          <td class="sk-table__cell">The rules, or the schema through a resolver such as Zod</td>
          <td class="sk-table__cell">Marking the control invalid and announcing why</td>
        </tr>
        <tr class="sk-table__row">
          <td class="sk-table__cell">When to validate and when to submit</td>
          <td class="sk-table__cell">The look of every control</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>One field</h2>
  <p>
    <code>ControlledField</code> is a field of the form drawn as a <code>FormField</code>. Its message
    comes from the schema, prints under the control, is announced when the control is focused, and
    marks the control invalid, with nothing to wire by hand.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={example} label="TSX" />

  <h2>Binding a control</h2>
  <p>
    Each control reports its value in its own shape. An adapter turns the field into the props that
    control expects, and the form holds exactly what the control reports, so the default values use
    the same shape.
  </p>
  <div class="sk-table-scroll">
    <table class="sk-table">
      <thead class="sk-table__head">
        <tr class="sk-table__row">
          <th class="sk-table__header">Control</th>
          <th class="sk-table__header">Adapter</th>
          <th class="sk-table__header">The form holds</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody class="sk-table__body">
        {
          bindings.map(([control, adapter, holds]) => (
            <tr class="sk-table__row">
              <td class="sk-table__cell">{control}</td>
              <td class="sk-table__cell"><code>{adapter}</code></td>
              <td class="sk-table__cell">{holds}</td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    A control no adapter fits receives the library's own <code>field</code> and <code>fieldState</code>
    untouched.
  </p>

  <h2>What you get without wiring</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>The error is the field's own, linked to the control through <code>aria-describedby</code>, and the control reads as invalid. Colour is never the only cue.</li>
    <li>Every adapter also hands the control the field's <code>id</code> and error wiring, so a <code>Checkbox</code> or a <code>Switch</code> is named by the field's label like an <code>Input</code> is.</li>
    <li>After a failed submit, focus goes to the first invalid field when that control exposes a focusable element. <code>Input</code>, <code>Checkbox</code> and <code>Switch</code> do; a control built on a popup does not, so check it.</li>
    <li>The browser's own validation bubbles are off, so only one validator speaks. The messages that remain are the field's.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    When a control carries its own text, such as a checkbox with a sentence beside it, both the field
    label and that text name it. Hide the field label with <code>labelVisuallyHidden</code>, or leave the
    control's text out.
  </p>

  <h2>Installing</h2>
  <p>
    Import from <code>@skryensya/react/hook-form</code>. <code>react-hook-form</code> is an optional peer
    dependency: it is only needed where you use this entry. The schema library and its resolver are
    yours to choose; nothing here knows which one is in use.
  </p>
</FoundationPage>
